  "summary_en": "UNESCO COMEST's 2017 report on robotics ethics (Latvian translation) surveys robot definitions, applications (industry, military, transport, health, education, home, agriculture), regulatory landscape and ethical challenges, and proposes values (dignity, autonomy, privacy, non-maleficence, responsibility, beneficence, justice) and thirteen recommendations, including human control over weapons and licensing of drones.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Distinguishes deterministic from cognitive robots; responsibility must remain with humans, not robots.",
   "Recommends ethics codes for roboticists and integrating ethics into engineering curricula and value-sensitive design.",
   "Recommends maintaining meaningful human control over weapon systems and states rethinking armed drone use.",
   "Recommends licensing private drones, public policies for police drones, and no weapons on police or domestic drones.",
   "Recommends defining situations where humans bear full responsibility for autonomous vehicle decisions, with insurance-type frameworks."
